#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
"""Are the live-deployed primary checkouts still on main? (substrate ARCH-437)

WHAT THIS PROTECTS. Several repos are deployed by being READ where they sit.
`~/.local/bin` and `~/.local/lib` symlink into agent-platform's working tree
(ARCH-229), orient's server renders from its checkout at request time (ARCH-182),
the knowledge watcher indexes the knowledge checkout, and the operator's lab publish runs
from the lab checkout. For all of them the branch the PRIMARY happens to sit on is
the code that actually runs — so a checkout left on a feature branch silently
serves un-merged work to every scheduled job on the box.

WHY A MONITOR, GIVEN ARCH-396 ALREADY INSTALLED A HOOK. Two hooks exist and
neither can close this. `pre-commit` refuses a commit made in the primary, which
does not fire on a pure branch switch. `post-checkout` catches the switch and
CANNOT BLOCK — git ignores its exit code — so it prints a warning into whichever
terminal ran the checkout. When that terminal belongs to an agent, the warning is
delivered and read by nobody, which is the same false-confidence shape as a Slack
post into a channel no one opens.

It has now happened twice. On 2026-07-22 agent-platform's primary sat on a deck
branch for a day while three merged PRs went unrun by the overnight; that incident
produced ARCH-396 and the hooks. On 2026-08-02 it sat on
`feat/session-view-deck-open-anchors` for seven minutes, was harmless only because
the branch pointer happened to equal main's head, and was noticed by a DIFFERENT co
session rather than by anything watching. A guard that depends on a human reading a
warning is not a guard; this is the check that does not.

BEHAVIOUR. A pass is silent. A breach logs an alert signal against the affected repo
(with dedup_key scoped to the UTC day, so a checkout left off main logs once a day
while it stays there, and again when it returns on a later day; one that returns
within the same UTC day logs nothing after that day's first signal, unless its key differs:
a job breach keys on its label, so a return with a different label logs again),
EXCEPT for a black-box crew — see below. A missing checkout degrades gracefully —
the repo is simply not checked. It never creates a work item (platform/decisions/0073).

A SECOND, INDEPENDENT BREACH: the job itself is not loaded or is disabled.
`com.convivy.appkit.autoupdate` sat disabled for 26 days before anyone noticed
(engineering-practice/skills/security-review-rubric rule 2) — a branch check can
never catch this, because a job that never fires also never leaves its checkout
on the wrong branch. `launchctl bootstrap` reports a disabled service only as an
opaque I/O error, so nothing short of asking `launchctl` directly surfaces it.
For each label declared in `_LIVE_CHECKOUTS[repo]["jobs"]`, `check_jobs` asks
`launchctl list <label>` (not loaded -> breach) against one `launchctl
print-disabled gui/<uid>` queried once for the whole sweep (listed disabled ->
breach; the query itself failing or printing something unparseable -> breach,
never a pass — a "disabled" that could not be checked must never read as
"enabled"). Logged on its own `dedup_key` (`primary-checkout:job:<repo>:<label>`),
separate from the branch check's (`primary-checkout:<repo>`), so neither
condition's signal can mask the other's.

A BLACK-BOX CREW'S BREACH IS NEVER LOGGED. ADR-0043 says "a black box crew files
no issues and logs no signals", and that rule is about the finding's EXISTENCE in
the shared, multi-reader signal store rather than about how much it says — so it
binds this check even though the only thing it ever reports is a branch name.
That is not incidental here: a private crew's branch name can itself name a
client or a deal. `main()` calls `_is_private_checkout` before building any
title, body, or dedup_key, on BOTH logging paths (the per-repo breach loop and the
unclassified audit), via the SAME registry lookup `check-serving-checkouts` and
`session_state.py` use — `private_crew.private_home_for`, not a second
implementation that could drift. A private crew's breach is still COUNTED toward
the exit code and still PRINTED in full to this process's own stderr; it simply
never reaches `signal_client`. So an operator reading this machine's own logs sees
everything, and the shared signal store sees nothing.

DELIBERATELY NOT SELF-HEALING. Switching a checkout back to main under whoever is
working in it would discard their context and could strand uncommitted work. The
correct actor is the person or agent that moved it, and the correct output is a
loud, durable alert naming what to run.

SCOPE. This checks the BRANCH only. Whether a checkout is BEHIND origin/main is a
different question with a different owner (`com.convivy.agent-platform-autopull`), and
folding the two would make one alert mean two things.

SAFETY. Read-only. Runs `git symbolic-ref` and nothing else; never checks out,
fetches, or writes to any repo.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime
import hashlib
import json
import os
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from typing import Any, NamedTuple
sys.path.insert(0, str(pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"))
import builder_root as BR  # noqa: E402

CONVIVY_ROOT = BR.root()

# The repos whose PRIMARY checkout is read live by something scheduled, and what
# reads it. DECLARED, not discovered: a repo that is merely present must not be
# policed, and only the deployment mechanism tells the two apart. The `reads` text
# goes into the alert, so whoever gets paged learns what is actually affected
# rather than only that a rule was broken.
#
# `jobs`: the launchd labels, among those already named in `reads` above, whose
# liveness `check_jobs` also checks. DECLARED here rather than parsed out of the
# `reads` prose at runtime, for the same reason the repo list itself is declared
# and not discovered: a rewording of `reads` that happened to drop a label's exact
# spelling would silently stop checking it, with nothing to say so. Left empty
# where `reads` names no single job: `agent-platform`'s "every cron job",
# `convivy-orient`'s `serve.sh`, and `selector`'s deliberately unenumerated
# `com.selector.*` family (see the comment at that entry for why).
_LIVE_CHECKOUTS: dict[str, dict[str, Any]] = {
    "agent-platform": {
        "reads": "~/.local/bin and ~/.local/lib symlink into this working tree "
                 "(ARCH-229), so every cron job and the 01:00 overnight run "
